commit 058f38858dc01c64f23233ceeb4716faf0b63d4e
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Thu Sep 3 17:03:53 2026 -0400

    Don't rely solely on pthread_cancel to stop AES-CTR-MT threads.
    
      Worker threads park in pthread_cond_wait() and, off of OSX, could only be
      removed by pthread_cancel(). When that cancel isn't delivered the
      following pthread_join() blocks forever and the session hangs at
      teardown. Reproduced under valgrind, which wedged roughly half of
      try-ciphers runs.
    
      The graceful exit path already existed for OSX, where cancels are also
      unreliable, but was fenced off there. Enable it everywhere and broadcast
      each queue's condition variable so parked threads wake, see the flag and
      exit. pthread_cancel stays as a fallback.

commit a430fc206ba44cbd628379074cd815277234c579
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Thu Sep 3 14:31:35 2026 -0400

    Make the valgrind PAM CI job actually test PAM.
    
      The valgrind-pam-1 job matched no branch in the inner case statement
      that assigns test sets so it ran with both LTESTS and SKIP_LTESTS empty,
      meaning the entire regress suite under valgrind. This ended up being consistently
      longer than the allocated 6 hours so the tests would also get cancled and
      never reach what we needed.
    
      Give valgrind-pam-1 its own test list covering authentication and
      session setup, and drop the dangling valgrind-pam pattern.
    
      While here, remove a duplicated EPHEMERAL_VM password-setup in
      setup_ci.sh.

